00:45:00,300 --> 00:45:03,640
I'm turning it into actually the largest number I can represent.

423
00:45:04,640 --> 00:45:06,400
And so it will be greater than zero.

424
00:45:07,660 --> 00:45:12,740
So it's a little bit weird to think about, I took an unsigned zero, which after all is just zero,

425
00:45:13,300 --> 00:45:21,220
and compared it to a negative 1, and it said, oh, you really meant 31, didn't you, or whatever word size we're using.

426
00:45:21,840 --> 00:45:23,040
And so it said it's greater.

427
00:45:25,160 --> 00:45:28,800
And basically, the rest of these, you can answer in a similar way.

428
00:45:30,980 --> 00:45:39,160
that these two numbers are, this is clearly one smaller than this.

429
00:45:39,160 --> 00:45:41,930
No, I'm sorry.

430
00:45:41,930 --> 00:45:50,930
This is a positive number, this is a negative number, and I compare those, and the positive will be greater than the negative.

431
00:45:50,930 --> 00:45:55,670
I'll tell you in a minute why this number is written this way.

432
00:45:55,670 --> 00:46:01,240
The next one, it's the same pair of numbers, but you see it's flipped.

433
00:46:01,240 --> 00:46:03,500
But you see it's flipped.

434
00:46:03,500 --> 00:46:04,480
And the reason is

435
00:46:04,500 --> 00:46:22,460
is that this number on the left is T max for, so it's the bit pattern in a 32-bit number.

436
00:46:22,460 --> 00:46:56,600
And to this day, I have not memorized this number, by the way. But, and then this number is actually

437
00:46:56,600 --> 00:47:24,460
T-min. And that when I subtract, you can think of it as this is negative T-max minus-1, so that's T-min.

438
00:47:24,460 --> 00:47:33,460
So if I do an unsigned comparison between those two, now think of this leading bit not being a negative weight, but a positive weight,

439
00:47:33,460 --> 00:47:36,460
you'll see that this is a bigger number than this one.

440
00:47:36,460 --> 00:47:49,220
And so it goes, that minus 1 is greater than minus 2 if they're unsigned,

441
00:47:49,220 --> 00:47:56,220
because minus 1 is, you can think of it as converts into U max, it will be the biggest number possible,

442
00:47:56,220 --> 00:48:00,110
and minus 2 will be, would be 1.

443
00:48:00,110 --> 00:48:17,890
would be one, one, one, one, zero.

444
00:48:17,890 --> 00:48:23,540
So that can get fairly confusing.

445
00:48:23,540 --> 00:48:27,540
One final quirk, this way of writing this number is,

446
00:48:27,540 --> 00:48:30,540
there's a little aside in the book about it.

447
00:48:30,540 --> 00:48:34,610
If you write this number, as you'd expect to,

448
00:48:34,610 --> 00:48:37,610
as minus blah, blah, blah, blah, blah,

449
00:48:37,610 --> 00:48:44,380
it will actually get, the C compiler gets kind of confused by that.

450
00:48:44,380 --> 00:48:47,600
by that for obscure reasons.

451
00:48:47,600 --> 00:48:54,600
One other thing I should point out that it's a property that I don't think we cover well enough here.

452
00:48:54,600 --> 00:48:57,070
Well, let me see.

453
00:48:57,070 --> 00:49:05,970
There's a few things that the slides don't really cover here, and I don't know why not,

454
00:49:05,970 --> 00:49:16,600
they like to point out, that are kind of useful properties to keep track of,

455
00:49:16,600 --> 00:49:33,230
is that you notice that if you look at the absolute value of T max,

456
00:49:33,230 --> 00:49:37,860
and you compare it to the absolute value of Tmin,

457
00:49:37,860 --> 00:49:47,340
that it's off by one, right?

458
00:49:47,340 --> 00:49:50,340
So that the negative number is more negative,

459
00:49:50,340 --> 00:49:52,340
the smallest negative number is more negative,

460
00:49:52,340 --> 00:49:56,400
is more negative than the largest positive number is positive.

461
00:49:56,400 --> 00:49:58,400
And the reason for that actually is fairly simple,

462
00:49:58,400 --> 00:50:01,400
if you think about, is that

463
00:50:01,400 --> 00:50:09,320
the cases where you have zeros include the number zero.

464
00:50:09,320 --> 00:50:26,490
You can see it better.

465
00:50:26,490 --> 00:50:29,490
And there's two to the half your range is

466
00:50:29,490 --> 00:50:36,670
occupied by those numbers and the negative numbers are all negative.

467
00:50:36,670 --> 00:50:46,790
And there's, so the other half of the range is in there.

468
00:50:46,790 --> 00:50:51,620
And the point is that you had to have to use one of these for zero.

469
00:50:51,620 --> 00:50:57,040
And so there's only, there's one left, one less value left over,

470
00:50:57,040 --> 00:51:01,040
and that's why you end up with this sort of asymmetry.

471
00:51:01,040 --> 00:51:06,550
And this asymmetry is the cause of no end of pain in various ways.

472
00:51:06,550 --> 00:51:34,250
ways that like if you, the classic example is if you implement absolute value like so,

473
00:51:34,250 --> 00:51:40,340
what do you, what does this return for T-min?

474
00:51:44,400 --> 00:51:45,400
Yeah?

475
00:51:45,400 --> 00:51:48,070
It returns T-min.

476
00:51:48,070 --> 00:51:49,070
We'll talk a little bit.

477
00:51:49,070 --> 00:52:08,280
Basically, when you try to negate this number that's here, you'll end up with it back again.

478
00:52:08,280 --> 00:52:09,280
So it's always a corner case.

479
00:52:09,280 --> 00:52:15,280
case. And whenever we do program testing, we always throw that case in there to break things.

480
00:52:15,280 --> 00:52:16,280
Question.

481
00:52:16,280 --> 00:52:24,620
You said, well, that case you said earlier, about how you had a writer as negative, something, something,

482
00:52:24,620 --> 00:52:25,620
minus one?

483
00:52:25,620 --> 00:52:26,620
Yes.

484
00:52:26,620 --> 00:52:27,620
Is that the number itself?

485
00:52:27,620 --> 00:52:28,620
Mm-hmm.

486
00:52:28,620 --> 00:52:29,620
Does that mess up?

487
00:52:29,620 --> 00:52:32,870
Does that like hate some weird bugs or anything?

488
00:52:32,870 --> 00:52:33,870
No.

489
00:52:33,870 --> 00:52:34,870
That's, they do that.

490
00:52:34,870 --> 00:52:41,610
If you ever look at the, in the length, in the C constants, they use this as a way of representing

491
00:52:41,610 --> 00:52:48,610
T-min and they do it for that reason because you see it's exactly this problem

492
00:52:48,610 --> 00:52:52,610
and they put parentheses around it so there's no precedence problem.

493
00:52:52,610 --> 00:52:57,930
But you see that number that in 647 that's T-4-7 that's T-max.

494
00:52:57,930 --> 00:53:04,930
And so basically it's just saying that T-min is minus T-max minus 1.

495
00:53:04,930 --> 00:53:17,360
Question?

496
00:53:17,360 --> 00:53:18,580
Yeah.

497
00:53:18,580 --> 00:53:19,580
Mm-hmm.

498
00:53:19,580 --> 00:53:20,580
Yes.

499
00:53:20,580 --> 00:53:22,560
So comparison is just a special case of the

500
00:53:22,580 --> 00:53:28,580
more general one, which is addition, subtraction, all of those.

501
00:53:28,580 --> 00:53:33,580
If either argument is unsigned, then it interprets the remaining one as being unsigned.

502
00:53:33,580 --> 00:53:39,870
It turns out, and we'll get into this more next time, as far as bit patterns,

503
00:53:39,870 --> 00:53:48,870
but it turns out that at the bit level, addition, subtraction, even multiplication,

504
00:53:48,870 --> 00:53:51,870
are the same whether it's an unsigned number or two's complement number.

505
00:53:51,870 --> 00:53:59,620
So we'll talk about that more next time.

506
00:53:59,620 --> 00:54:19,000
And the other thing to observe, sort of property, is you saw that UMAX is

507
00:54:19,000 --> 00:54:31,210
you saw that UMAX is sort of like twice Tmax plus 1.

508
00:54:31,210 --> 00:54:32,750
Right?

509
00:54:32,750 --> 00:54:39,520
So before UMAX was 15 in our 5-bit numbers.

510
00:54:39,520 --> 00:54:43,740
And that's, again, not too hard to figure out.

511
00:54:43,740 --> 00:54:44,740
Here's one way to think about.

512
00:54:44,740 --> 00:54:47,360
Here's one way to think about it.

513
00:54:47,360 --> 00:54:53,220
T-max is a zero followed by a bunch of ones.

514
00:54:53,220 --> 00:55:01,700
And if I want to double that number, I basically shift it left by a one position.

515
00:55:01,700 --> 00:55:05,000
And that would end up with a zero here.

516
00:55:05,000 --> 00:55:13,010
So this is T-max, and this is twice T-max.

517
00:55:13,010 --> 00:55:18,700
And now if I add a one to that, I'll just set this bit to one and get this.

518
00:55:18,700 --> 00:55:22,000
So there's various ways you can think about it, but that's one way to do it.

519
00:55:22,000 --> 00:55:47,010
There's one other thing that I wanted to mention too.

520
00:55:47,010 --> 00:55:53,140
This can get you into a lot of trouble programming-wise.

521
00:55:53,140 --> 00:55:57,670
So let me just show you what I mean.

522
00:55:57,670 --> 00:56:15,760
So imagine I wanted to go backwards through an array.

523
00:56:15,760 --> 00:56:25,760
So I write a loop of the form for i equals n minus 1, i is greater than or equal to

524
00:56:25,760 --> 00:56:37,180
or equal to 0, I minus minus, and then I do something with array element A of I.

525
00:56:37,180 --> 00:56:44,020
I'll just call some function I.

526
00:56:44,020 --> 00:56:45,020
Whatever you want to do in that loop.

527
00:56:45,020 --> 00:56:48,020
So that's a pretty standard pattern in a loop, right?

528
00:56:48,020 --> 00:56:53,210
Except that you write this greater equal to one, zero.

529
00:56:53,210 --> 00:56:57,080
You can imagine various uses for that.

530
00:56:57,080 --> 00:57:00,300
So here's an interesting question.

531
00:57:00,300 --> 00:57:04,870
What if I were declared as being unsigned?

532
00:57:04,870 --> 00:57:11,400
unsigned because it's a ray index after all.

533
00:57:11,400 --> 00:57:14,920
What will happen with this loop?

534
00:57:14,920 --> 00:57:22,980
Hmm? Yeah? What's that? Yes. So the loop will go forever. What would most

535
00:57:22,980 --> 00:57:29,790
likely happen is I would go from being zero to being u max and that's a really big

536
00:57:29,790 --> 00:57:38,490
number and the ray you'd most likely cause a memory fault because it's so far out

537
00:57:38,490 --> 00:57:42,910
of bounds. But the point is it wouldn't do what you want. It wouldn't stop. It wouldn't stop.

538
00:57:42,930 --> 00:57:44,430
where you want it to.

539
00:57:44,430 --> 00:57:47,930
And because of the simple reason that I is, of course,

540
00:57:47,930 --> 00:57:53,190
I is always going to be greater or equal to zero because it's an unsigned number.

541
00:57:53,190 --> 00:58:00,310
So that's one you'd say, okay, well, you should have figured that one out.

542
00:58:00,310 --> 00:58:10,660
It can be fair, more subtle though, if you have something like int I,

543
00:58:10,660 --> 00:58:20,500
and then we say something like, let me just change it here a little bit.

544
00:58:20,500 --> 00:58:46,190
So this will be something like this where I'm mixing metaphors here, I have to confess.

545
00:58:46,190 --> 00:58:56,190
But if you use the standard term size of, which is the way you get how many bytes it takes to represent some given data type,

546
00:58:56,190 --> 00:58:59,480
so this will actually be the number one.

547
00:58:59,480 --> 00:59:08,480
So it's not the same loop as before, but imagine I'm sort of mixing in my arithmetic and making use of some of these built-in forms.

548
00:59:08,480 --> 00:59:11,080
What would happen?

549
00:59:11,080 --> 00:59:15,000
with this loop. Yes.

550
00:59:15,000 --> 00:59:20,870
Right.

551
00:59:20,870 --> 00:59:21,870
Yes.

552
00:59:21,870 --> 00:59:22,870
Exactly.

553
00:59:22,870 --> 00:59:26,870
So size of returns an unsigned value.

554
00:59:26,870 --> 00:59:27,870
It's not actually a function.

555
00:59:27,870 --> 00:59:30,870
It's a built-in, but it's the result of size of

556
00:59:30,870 --> 00:59:32,870
is considered to be unsigned.

557
00:59:32,870 --> 00:59:36,960
And so, just like I was saying,

558
00:59:36,960 --> 00:59:41,280
if you have a sign value in an unsigned

559
00:59:41,280 --> 00:59:44,280
in any kind of arithmetic, it'll

560
00:59:44,280 --> 00:59:46,280
treat the combination of the two as unsigned.

561
00:59:46,280 --> 00:59:54,600
the two is unsigned and so it will actually do an unsigned comparison here, which is almost surely not what you wanted.

562
00:59:54,600 --> 01:00:02,600
And this will just, you'll look at this program and you won't have any idea why it's crashing.

563
01:00:02,600 --> 01:00:04,600
You'll be so confused.

564
01:00:04,600 --> 01:00:06,600
And that's the kind of thing you have to know.

565
01:00:06,600 --> 01:00:11,600
It's one of the quirks of C that probably if they could start over again and redesign it,

566
01:00:11,600 --> 01:00:14,600
they'd come up with some different set of conventions than they did.

567
01:00:14,600 --> 01:00:17,600
But unfortunately, it's way too late for that.

568
01:00:17,600 --> 01:00:21,490
This is just one of those things you have to remember.

569
01:00:21,490 --> 01:00:28,370
But yes, my point is there's some subtleties in C about this business of sign versus unsigned

570
01:00:28,370 --> 01:00:36,150
arithmetic.

571
01:00:36,150 --> 01:00:37,150
Okay.

572
01:00:37,150 --> 01:00:45,070
So now let's look at a sort of final class of operations, which is, suppose I have a number that's so many

573
01:00:45,070 --> 01:00:51,770
bits, say eight bits, and I want to now expand it to be a 16-bit number.

574
01:00:51,770 --> 01:00:55,130
There's a fairly cute rule for doing this.

575
01:00:55,150 --> 01:00:56,770
that's called sign extension.

576
01:00:56,770 --> 01:01:00,430
I'm talking about two complement numbers here.

577
01:01:00,430 --> 01:01:08,540
Of how you go take a number and make it bigger without changing its value.

578
01:01:08,540 --> 01:01:15,410
And the basic rule is you do it by copying the sign bit to the left.

579
01:01:15,410 --> 01:01:30,110
So let me give you the intuition behind that.

580
01:01:30,110 --> 01:01:42,190
So let's look at some four bit values.

581
01:01:42,190 --> 01:01:46,800
Well, it's pretty straightforward if the leading bit is a zero.

582
01:01:46,800 --> 01:01:51,840
I'm just going to do a left shift and then fill in.

583
01:01:51,840 --> 01:01:59,540
So, right.

584
01:01:59,540 --> 01:02:04,770
Wait, what am I talking about?

585
01:02:04,770 --> 01:02:05,770
I got ahead of myself.

586
01:02:05,770 --> 01:02:07,130
I'm talking about something totally different.

587
01:02:07,130 --> 01:02:08,330
No, sorry.

588
01:02:08,330 --> 01:02:11,510
The same number, I'm not changing the number, I'm keeping it the same.

589
01:02:11,510 --> 01:02:16,270
So I just add a zero to the lead and this will be the number six still.

590
01:02:16,270 --> 01:02:22,030
This is plus four plus two equals six.

591
01:02:22,030 --> 01:02:29,100
But now let's think about it as a where there's a leading sign bit.

592
01:02:29,100 --> 01:02:30,500
So this is

593
01:02:30,520 --> 01:02:37,770
minus 8 plus 4 plus 2 is equal to minus 2.

594
01:02:37,770 --> 01:02:44,960
So now look at what happens if I copy that sign bit over one position.

595
01:02:44,960 --> 01:02:55,260
So this becomes a 1-1-1-0.

596
01:02:55,260 --> 01:03:02,000
And what happens is this is this is a now has value plus 8 here,

597
01:03:02,000 --> 01:03:05,000
but the sign bit has value minus 16.

598
01:03:05,000 --> 01:03:10,560
and then this is still plus 2 and plus 4.

599
01:03:10,560 --> 01:03:12,560
And so it will still equal minus 2.

600
01:03:12,560 --> 01:03:15,330
So here's what happened.

601
01:03:15,330 --> 01:03:20,910
Before my sign bit had a weight of minus 8.

602
01:03:20,910 --> 01:03:24,360
And my new sign bit has a weight of minus 16.

603
01:03:24,360 --> 01:03:29,680
But I converted that old sign bit into a positive number, which is plus 8.

604
01:03:29,680 --> 01:03:31,840
And those two cancelled out.

605
01:03:31,840 --> 01:03:34,000
Right?

606
01:03:34,000 --> 01:03:38,000
You combine those two and you get minus 8, which is exactly this.

607
01:03:38,000 --> 01:03:45,670
is exactly this. So you see by sort of copying that sign bit over, giving it twice

608
01:03:45,670 --> 01:03:53,670
the way turning it what was the sign bit into a positive number, you don't change the net effect of the sum.

609
01:03:53,670 --> 01:04:00,670
And that's exactly the idea of sign extension. Just keep going with that as far as you need to carry it over.

610
01:04:00,670 --> 01:04:07,670
And each time you're doing it, you're effectively doing this business of coming up with a pair of bits,

611
01:04:07,670 --> 01:04:14,010
of bits that end up with the same value as this original bit.

612
01:04:14,010 --> 01:04:22,920
So that's the idea of sign extension and you'll see that a lot in a bit patterns, especially if

613
01:04:22,920 --> 01:04:38,720
you see a bit pattern with that starts with a bunch of f's, what that tells you, remember,

614
01:04:38,720 --> 01:04:49,740
f is just four ones, is a whole bunch of leading ones. That tells you the number is negative.

615
01:04:49,740 --> 01:04:53,360
Negative, but not too far away from zero.

616
01:04:53,760 --> 01:04:54,760
Right?

617
01:04:54,760 --> 01:04:56,620
And you'll see that over and over again.

618
01:04:56,620 --> 01:04:58,960
You'll see bit patterns and you just sort of want to,

619
01:04:58,960 --> 01:05:01,600
when you see leading Fs, you say, oh, that's a negative number.

620
01:05:01,600 --> 01:05:07,490
I know what's going on there.

621
01:05:07,490 --> 01:05:11,270
And so here's some examples, but like, you can see,

622
01:05:11,270 --> 01:05:16,600
the main idea is you carry that leading bit from the 16-bit case

623
01:05:16,600 --> 01:05:25,400
over and make as many copies as you need to expand the word size.

624
01:05:25,400 --> 01:05:29,400
And I should mention with the unsigned case, obviously,

625
01:05:29,400 --> 01:05:31,560
you just want to fill in with zeros.

626
01:05:31,800 --> 01:05:38,660
Do we talk about truncation?

627
01:05:38,660 --> 01:05:42,270
It's sort of missing slides in this, but...

628
01:05:42,270 --> 01:05:54,380
A final case to look at is, what happens if I make something shorter?

629
01:05:54,380 --> 01:05:59,780
So if I had my unsigned number from before,

630
01:05:59,780 --> 01:06:21,920
if this were an unsigned number, this would be 1, 2, 8, 16, 14, 17, right?

631
01:06:21,920 --> 01:06:24,920
What if I decided, oh, I can't afford a...

632
01:06:24,920 --> 01:06:29,160
four bit.

633
01:06:29,160 --> 01:06:31,160
I don't have enough money for five bits.

634
01:06:31,160 --> 01:06:34,030
I'm only going to give you four.

635
01:06:34,030 --> 01:06:36,030
Well, you basically just drop the most significant bit.

636
01:06:36,030 --> 01:06:42,490
And you get 8 plus 2 plus 1,

637
01:06:42,490 --> 01:06:44,490
which is 11, which is 9.

638
01:06:44,490 --> 01:06:50,350
And the relation there is that this is,

639
01:06:50,350 --> 01:06:52,350
it's like modular arithmetic.

640
01:06:52,350 --> 01:07:00,010
Right? Mod 16.

641
01:07:00,010 --> 01:07:02,110
You're taking...

642
01:07:02,110 --> 01:07:08,330
No? Thank you.

643
01:07:08,330 --> 01:07:14,300
It's the risk of improvising.

644
01:07:14,300 --> 01:07:15,300
So, um, 27.

645
01:07:15,300 --> 01:07:26,590
27 and 11.

646
01:07:26,590 --> 01:07:31,470
Yeah, that makes sense.

647
01:07:31,470 --> 01:07:32,470
The difference is 16.

648
01:07:32,470 --> 01:07:35,080
I knew that.

649
01:07:35,080 --> 01:07:38,080
Okay. Excuse me for my stumbling.

650
01:07:38,080 --> 01:07:41,080
But the point being that when you drop this thing,

651
01:07:41,080 --> 01:07:46,080
effectively you're just taking the mod some power of 2 of it.

652
01:07:46,080 --> 01:07:50,080
Like I mentioned the case where you're taking module 8.

653
01:07:50,080 --> 01:07:54,080
If you just keep only the lowest sort of three bits, you're having a module 8.

654
01:07:54,080 --> 01:07:56,080
Now, one really, this is sort of,

655
01:07:56,080 --> 01:07:59,080
sort of logical in the unsigned world.

656
01:07:59,080 --> 01:08:03,080
Modular arithmetic is something that was understood

657
01:08:03,080 --> 01:08:05,080
by the ancient Greeks.

658
01:08:05,080 --> 01:08:13,360
So not too far away from human experience.

659
01:08:13,360 --> 01:08:15,360
But it gets a little bit funky

660
01:08:15,360 --> 01:08:24,270
if you're dealing with two complement numbers

661
01:08:24,270 --> 01:08:26,270
because this is now

662
01:08:26,270 --> 01:08:32,280
10, 11, minus,

663
01:08:32,280 --> 01:08:41,460
five, right? But this number, if I just dropped this bit, and call it a 4-bit number,

664
01:08:41,460 --> 01:08:51,610
this now becomes minus 8, 2, and 1, which is minus 5.

665
01:08:51,610 --> 01:08:58,640
You'd say, how'd that happen?

666
01:08:58,640 --> 01:09:04,850
Well, actually, if you look at it, this is just what you'd get by sign extension, right?

667
01:09:04,850 --> 01:09:12,270
So this wasn't a very interesting example.

668
01:09:12,270 --> 01:09:29,450
But if we changed it to this, then this would be minus 13, and this would be plus

669
01:09:29,450 --> 01:09:41,640
3 and it's a little harder to think about in terms of modular arithmetic.

670
01:09:41,640 --> 01:09:48,830
The way you basically have to think about it is, well, you know, this is minus 13, but it's really kind of like 27,

671
01:09:48,830 --> 01:09:58,830
and 27 mod 16 is sort of like, or no, this isn't 20, we changed our number, right?

672
01:09:58,830 --> 01:10:04,830
So this is really kind of like 19, and 19 mod 16 is 3, and then.

673
01:10:04,830 --> 01:10:11,130
And this is three. So you can sort of do it by coming back around to it, but it's

674
01:10:11,130 --> 01:10:17,820
not some arithmetic property that would jump out at you as being something logical.

675
01:10:17,820 --> 01:10:19,860
But that happens all the time.

676
01:10:19,860 --> 01:10:25,880
And it's very common for numbers to get truncated for one reason or another.

677
01:10:25,880 --> 01:10:30,160
And you can see that you can have a negative number become a positive number.

678
01:10:30,160 --> 01:10:34,420
You can have a positive number become a negative number and all kinds of goofy stuff can happen.

679
01:10:34,420 --> 01:10:39,380
And we'll see plenty of examples of that when we look at arithmetic.

680
01:10:39,380 --> 01:10:48,180
So that's, that is what I want to cover for today.

681
01:10:48,180 --> 01:10:52,180
And we'll cover the other arithmetic on Tuesday.

682
01:10:52,180 --> 01:10:54,180
So thank you very much.

